= Version control — Simple =

'''Version control''' records how files change over time.

It lets people keep a history instead of only keeping the newest copy.

With version control, you can often:

* see what changed;
* compare old and new versions;
* restore an earlier version;
* see who recorded a change;
* work on a separate branch;
* combine work from different branches.

Version control is very common in [[Software/Simple|software]] development, but it can also track documentation, configuration, websites, research code, and other files.

Git is one version-control system. It is not the same thing as version control itself.

Version control also does not prove that a newer version is correct. It records changes; review and evidence are separate.

